%T A399633 2,2,3,1,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,11,1,2,2,3,2,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,10,1,2,
%U A399633 2,3,1,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,11,1,2,2,3
%N A399633 The city-building sequence: a(n) = the height h of the shortest building which can see exactly h earlier buildings.
%C A399633 A building a(j) "sees" an earlier building a(i) if every intermediate building a(k), i < k < j, has its top (k, a(k)) on or below the straight line joining (i, a(i)) and (j, a(j)).
%C A399633 If at some point there doesn't exist a next height h at which the condition is satisfied, the sequence is finite. The sequence has at least 2.3 billion terms.
%C A399633 If the sequence is infinite, does every number eventually appear? The earliest appearance of height h is A399763(h). For record heights and when they appear, see A399949 and A399950.
%C A399633 The apparent "spikes" at n = 16k for k = 1..7 (10, 10, 11, 10, 11, 10, 19) follow a pattern similar to the values at n for n = 2..8 (1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 3). Similar temporary analogies seem to emerge at various scales, but do any last indefinitely?
%C A399633 From _Rémy Sigrist_, Sep 16 2026: (Start)
%C A399633 The sequence is infinite: suppose that we have n > 0 terms:
%C A399633 - let v(k) be the number of buildings, among the first n buildings, visible from a building of height k >= 0 at position n+1,
%C A399633 - v(0) > 0 as we see the building at position n from the ground,
%C A399633 - v(k) = n for k sufficiently large as we see all the buildings from a point high enough,
%C A399633 - v(k) <= k for k sufficiently large,
%C A399633 - let h be the least k such that v(k) <= k (note that h > 0 as v(0) > 0),
%C A399633 - v(h-1) > h-1 by necessity; equivalently v(h-1) >= h,
%C A399633 - as v is weakly increasing, v(h) >= v(h-1) >= h,
%C A399633 - so v(h) = h, and a(n+1) = h.
%C A399633 The sequence b(n) = n-1 corresponds to the variant with the highest buildings.
%C A399633 The sequence is unbounded: by contradiction:
%C A399633 - boundedness implies a finite number of values,
%C A399633 - as the sequence is infinite, at least one value appears infinitely many times,
%C A399633 - let m be the greatest such value,
%C A399633 - a(n) <= m for n sufficiently large, say for n > s,
%C A399633 - the (m+2)-th occurrence of m beyond s sees the m+1 prior m's: a contradiction.
%C A399633 The k terms following a record high point, say a(n) >= k*(k-1), are independent of n: a(n+i) = a(i) + 1 for i = 1..k.
%C A399633 The sequence contains every integer: a(1) = 0; if v is a term, then v+1 is also a term.
%C A399633 Thanks to _Ivan N. Ianakiev_ for our discussions, which led to these comments.
%C A399633 (End)
%H A399633 Eric Fox, <a href="/A399633/b399633.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399633 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399633/a399633.png">Colored scatterplot of the sequence for n = 1..2500</a> (black pixels correspond to the terms of the sequence; blue/white/red pixels correspond to places (n, h) <> (n, a(n)) where the number of visible building respectively is less than/equals/is more than h)
%H A399633 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399633/a399633_1.png">Colored scatterplot of the sequence for n = 1..2500</a> (black pixels correspond to the terms of the sequence; colored pixels correspond to the proportion of buildings visible from (n, h) <> (n, a(n)))
%H A399633 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399633/a399633.txt">C# program</a>
%H A399633 N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="/A399633/a399633_2.jpg">Illustration of a(1)-a(18)</a>
%e A399633 For n = 8:
%e A399633 A height of 0 would see a(6)=2 and a(7)=1, but must see 0 other buildings.
%e A399633 A height of 1 would see a(6)=2 and a(7)=1, but must see 1 other building.
%e A399633 A height of 2 would see a(4)=2, a(6)=2, and a(7)=1, but must see 2 other buildings.
%e A399633 A height of 3 sees a(4)=2, a(6)=2, and a(7)=1, exactly 3 other buildings, so a(8)=3.
%e A399633                   *
%e A399633           *   *   *
%e A399633       * * * * * * *
%e A399633 n = 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
%o A399633 (C#) // See Links section.
%o A399633 (Python)
%o A399633 from itertools import count
%o A399633 def A399633_generator():
%o A399633     a_list = []
%o A399633     while 1:
%o A399633         for a in count():
%o A399633             c = dx0 = dy0 = 0
%o A399633             for dx,y in enumerate(reversed(a_list),1):
%o A399633                 dy = y-a
%o A399633                 if dx0*dy >= dx*dy0:
%o A399633                     c += 1
%o A399633                     if c > a: break
%o A399633                     dx0,dy0 = dx,dy
%o A399633             else:
%o A399633                 if c == a: break
%o A399633         yield a
%o A399633         a_list.append(a) # _Pontus von Brömssen_, Sep 24 2026
%Y A399633 Cf. A399763 (indices of the first appearances of each height).
%Y A399633 There are numerous sequences derived from this one. For convenience, here is a list as of Sep 22, 2026: A397493, A397831, A398355, A399194, A399763, A399949, A399950, A399977, A400110, A400111, A400133, A400183, A400184, A400185.
